q192-multiples: B agrees and independent b1=13,b4=52,b7=91. C's ceiling bound gives104; D reverses the seven multiples. A's stated derivation is internally inconsistent: "discard13 because its multiplier is the one-digit number1" cannot justify retaining multipliers2..7, which are also one-digit. A is genuinely false, but its proposed misconception does not generate the offered six-term sequence. Mathematics verdict REVISE for this explanation, not for the key.
q192-parity: B agrees numerically. A flips the parity classes; C makes all terms after the first constant0 and fails at253. These are false plausible procedures. Fidelity/leakage findings from phase2 survive: no actual parity application is elicited. The intent's "evaluations logically retained" does not establish that a learner must make those distinctions.
q193-neighbors: C agrees; A's +2 successor and B's reversed endpoints produce the stated errors. All internal predecessors are correct, but all visible alternatives share the identical predecessor sentence. Declared writing loss does not account for the absent source(b) distinction. Phase2 fidelity/leakage failures survive.
q194-successor: C agrees; direction reversal, value+1, doubled index yield A,B,D. Whole-index expansion matches all five independent expressions; value+1 disproved by constant sequence. k>=3 is sufficient and necessary for the simultaneous supplied terms.
q194-predecessor: A agrees; successor, value-1, skip-two produce B,C,D. All five expanded indices agree, including2k+2 andk-5. k>=6 correctly preserves positive predecessors. D can also givec0 forj=2.
q195-interior: B agrees. A includes both endpoints; C includes only right endpoints. All three ranges correspond exactly to the phase1 interior sets.
q196-reverse-index: D agrees. A movesk rather thank-1 positions back; B movesforward; C treats reverse ordinal as forward. Counterexamples in phase2 establish false general rules even if some particular n,k coincide.
q197-increasing: B,D agree. A compares decimal-place count; C denominator growth; E absolute magnitudes; F nondecreasing instead ofstrict. Each wrong procedure explains selecting its false sequence.
q197-decreasing: A,C agree. B treats extra precision as value reduction; D attends only to denominator growth; E endpoint-only comparison; F complements strict increase. Actual adjacent values refute all four.
q198-examples: A,C agree. A has increments+1,-1,0; C +1,-3,alternating. B permits equality asincrease; D orders negative magnitudes rather than signed values. All rationale computations agree. Grouped response permits elimination on one incorrect component; this is not automatically a failure. The nonunique constant and oscillating third-category witnesses remain meaningful, though separate production/testing of each category is not demonstrated.
q199-round-seven: B agrees. Truncation producesA, ceiling producesC, first-downward-step classification producesD. All numbers and misconceptions match the wrong procedures; no math/key defect. Coverage comparison exposed an omission in the prekey fidelity judgment: EVERY alternative says "no equal terms"; the requested repetition inquiry is thus never distinguished and its answer is jointly disclosed. All alternatives also supply the same infinite decimal. The conversion-production loss is acceptable as prerequisite scaffolding, but absence of the repeated-term distinction is not merely writing loss. Final fidelity/leakage verdicts REVISE. This later finding is recorded here; phase2-prekey.record was not retroactively changed or backdated.
q199-round-twentythree: C agrees. A counts the integer0 as an extra precision digit; B assumes extra precision decreasesvalue. Both derivations explain the false alternatives. The infinite expansion is supplied identically in every option, so conversion is not independently assessed; precise index and trend distinctions survive. This is a nonessential prerequisite/production limitation, not a mathematical key defect.
q200-descending-graph: A agrees. B swaps coordinates, C shifts tozero, D interpolates (1.5,4). All sixpoints and discrete-domain fact match phase1. No drawing or rendered-graph recognition claimed.
q200-irregular-graph: C agrees. A shifts tozero; B sorts thevalues; D connects points. Every point, zeroordinate and negativeordinate agree with phase1; sorted option is a different function.
q201-graph-classification: A agrees. All8 truths agree with actual a,b,c,d values, including c7=4 and d4=-.5. Local-branch, final-step, local-rebound, absolute-magnitude and omitted-point misconceptions all explain false class claims. Empty decreasing subset is honestly retained.
q202-rectangles: A agrees. Positive sides6,14 establish84; sum20 maxarea100 rules out105. B's mistaken sum=p instead ofp/2 produces max400 and would wronglyaccept105, so the misconception is concrete and relevant.
q203-sum: A,C agree. B line-intercept-only ignores the quadratic; D copies5 into y instead of5-x. Equation and sum arithmetic matches phase2; bothroots retained.
q203-double: B,D agree. A incorrectly negatesroot2 despite linear8x; C solvesx+10=0 withwrong sign. Both false points obey proportionality but fail the equation; both valid roots retained.

